    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey can u explain how lactic acid actually works in your muscles during high intensity training? like why does it make them burn and how does the body get rid of it after the workout is over?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sure! During high-intensity efforts, your muscles break down glucose for energy faster than oxygen can be supplied, so they rely on **anaerobic glycolysis**, which produces lactate (often called lactic acid) along with hydrogen ions. Contrary to old beliefs, lactate itself isn't the culprit — it's actually the accumulating **hydrogen ions** that lower muscle pH and create that burning sensation and fatigue.\n\nAfter exercise, your body clears lactate via the **Cori cycle**, where it travels through the blood to the liver and gets converted back into glucose, plus your muscles and heart can use it directly as fuel. The **afterburn effect**: elevated oxygen consumption post-workout helps metabolize it, so lactate typically clears within 30–60 minutes — it's not the cause of next-day soreness (that's DOMS, from micro-damage).",
      "source": "target",
